## What was still broken

Its mirror image, which nothing guarded: a re-paste of any item back into the parent leaves the size gate green (121 lines of headroom) and clippy green. Two live definitions in two live modules isn't an error — it's a duplicate, the same copy-not-move shape #805 was filed about.

## The deliverable: a guard, not an edit

One new test (+155 lines, one file) asserting each of the six items is defined exactly once across all of src/ and that the one site is the child. Needles are definition-shaped, not bare names, because the parent legitimately names three of them in its re-export.

I deleted my own second guard rather than keep it. A the_parent_names_the_child_module test stood in the first draft; I measured that breaking the mod line alone already fails cargo build with E0432 naming the parent's import — so it could never redden alone, which makes it ceremony. Its assertion survives inside the remaining test as the anti-vacuous precondition.

Five controls, all serial and atomic, every restore confirmed byte-identical: both needles redden the count assertion by name, the mod needle reddens the precondition, the include_str! misdirection reddens the anti-vacuous pointer, and a planted compile_error! makes cargo build name the module — which is the compiled vs merely present distinction the receipt turned on.

Gates: fmt, clippy -D warnings, build all clean; cargo test 5969 passed / 0 failed; module_size (28) agrees with the wc -l; orphan_modules (11) green. No pre-existing test touched. Committed as 85d8ff04; per-file history in ARCHITECTURE.md; #805 closed with the measurements.

One honest caveat I'd flag rather than bury: my guard asserts on text, so a renamed duplicate or one inlined at a call site slips past it. That's stated in the guard's own doc comment and in the architecture record — it's a narrowing that catches the shape #805 was actually filed about, not general duplicate detection.
